<h3 class="about-title order-title">ABOUT</h3>
<hr class="order-rule">

<p class="about-text">
The Order of the Lion's Bell is an independent philosophical, research, and chivalric fellowship devoted to the pursuit and preservation of knowledge. We investigate unusual phenomena, consciousness, history, philosophy, psychical research, UAP, folklore, and other questions which remain uncertain, neglected, or difficult to place within a single field. Members of the Order are known as <span class="accent">Heralds of the Lion</span>.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

<br><br>


<h3 class="about-title order-title">THE WORK OF THE ORDER</h3>
<hr class="order-rule">

<p class="about-text">
Heralds collect books, documents, testimony, correspondence, observations, archival material, and reports. Open Case Files where questions deserve investigation, compare claims with the evidence offered for them, preserve unusual human experiences and publish useful findings through Bell Reports and <em>The Bell</em>. This work is maintained through the <span class="accent">Heralds Research System</span>, which joins our Research Library, Experience Archive, cases, claims, evidence, correspondence, and continuing investigations into a common record.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

<br><br>


<h3 class="about-title order-title">WHY AN ORDER?</h3>
<hr class="order-rule">

<p class="about-text">
We call ourselves an Order because investigation is not our only concern. The Lion's Bell is also a fellowship shaped by a Rule, common study, offices, gatherings, stages of formation, correspondence, and service to one another. From the chivalric tradition we take the ideas of courage, discipline, loyalty, restraint, responsibility, and the obligation to place one's abilities in the service of something beyond oneself. A title in the Order is therefore not an ornament. It carries work, resposibility and duty with it.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
A Herald is both investigator and messenger. We go looking for the questions others overlook, neglect, or consider too strange to ask. Knowledge belongs to no caste or profession alone. Scholar and amateur, witness and archivist, scientist and historian may each possess one part of a larger problem. <span class="accent">The Bell sounds for all.</span>
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

<br><br>


<h3 class="about-title order-title">TRADITION</h3>
<hr class="order-rule">

<p class="about-text">
The Order draws from older traditions concerned with knowledge, initiation, philosophy and preservation. They form a body of symbols, stories, texts, and examples through which the Order understands its own work.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


<p class="about-subtitle">
Sais and the Keepers of Records
</p>

<p class="about-text">
In Plato's <em>Timaeus</em> and <em>Critias</em>, Solon travels to Egypt and speaks with priests at Sais who tell him that the Greeks have repeatedly lost the memory of their own past while Egyptian records preserved accounts reaching much further back. 
</p>

<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


<p class="about-subtitle">
The Pythagorean Influence
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Pythagoras remains one of the great philosophical influences of the Order. The Pythagorean tradition joined the investigation of nature and number with philosophy, fellowship, discipline, and the formation of the individual. 
</p>

<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


<p class="about-subtitle">
Hermes and the Herald
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Hermes is the messenger, traveller, interpreter, and mediator. The later figure of Hermes Trismegistus and the Hermetic writings became important influences upon philosophy, cosmology, alchemy, and Western esoteric thought. These traditions remain part of the philosophical inheritance studied by the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The name <span class="accent">Herald</span> carries the same idea into our own work. A Herald brings reports in and carries knowledge outward.
</p>
<p class="about-text">
Hermes also possesses another character important to the investigator: the Trickster. Writers on folklore, psychology, religion, and anomalous phenomena have repeatedly encountered the same association with ambiguity, inversion, coincidence, deception, contradiction, and the crossing of boundaries. 
</p>

<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>

<br><br>


<h3 class="about-title order-title">THE LION, THE BELL & THE SUN</h3>
<hr class="order-rule">

<p class="about-text">
The <span class="accent">Lion</span> is the emblem of courage, guardianship, and responsibility. It has appeared under many forms throughout religion, philosophy, heraldry, alchemy, and esoteric tradition. From Sekhmet and Chnoubis to the Green and Red Lions. 
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The <span class="accent">Sun</span> and the <span class="accent">Bell</span> express two forms of revelation: light and sound. The Sun illuminates what was hidden from sight. The Bell announces what must be heard. Together they represent discovery and proclamation, to find knowledge and then to herald it.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Bell is also a call to gather and a measure of passing time. Its sound reaches outward without distinction. King and beggar, initiate and stranger, scholar and amateur hear the same bell and live beneath the same sun. In this sense the motto <em>Campana sonat omnibus</em> — <span class="accent">the Bell sounds for all</span>  is also a reminder of equality and mortality. Our time is brief. Whatever can be learned, preserved, and passed onward must be done while the flame still burns.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
This is the purpose of the Herald: to seek what is hidden, preserve what would otherwise be lost, test what can be tested, and carry forward what is worth knowing.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


<p class="about-quote">
<em>Campana sonat omnibus.</em><br>
<span>The Bell sounds for all.</span>
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>


<p class="about-quote">
ὁ ἔχων ὦτα ἀκούειν ἀκουέτω<br>
<span>"He who has ears to hear, let him hear."</span>
</p>


</div>
</section>
</main>


